<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zheng He</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1371">1371</a>–c. 1433</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zheng He was a Chinese admiral and court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> who led seven great maritime expeditions for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> between 1405 and 1433. His fleets of large treasure ships sailed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, establishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a> and projecting imperial prestige. Accounts of the Ming Empire treat the voyages as a high point of Ming maritime power, though they were later discontinued.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-he.jpg" alt="Zheng He" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin Konsek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016_Malakka,_Stadhuys_(09).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mogadishu and Barawa were also visited by the Ming fleets of <b>Zheng He</b> in the early fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The fleet of <b>Zheng He</b> called in 1414, and Malindi's ruler sent a personal envoy with a giraffe as a present to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malindi">Malindi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Domestic markets thrived, and in the early 15th century the Yongle Emperor sponsored <b>Zheng He</b>'s voyages to Southeast Asia, India and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early fifteenth century the Ming admiral <b>Zheng He</b> led seven great expeditions across the Indian Ocean, as far as the coast of East Africa, with fleets far larger than any Europe could then have assembled.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hongwu Emperor forbade them to read or to meddle in politics, but from Yongle’s reign they ran huge imperial workshops, commanded armies, went as envoys and took part in appointing and promoting officials; the eunuch <b>Zheng He</b> led seven great voyages into the Indian Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li><li><blockquote>An imperial edict addressed to <b>Zheng He</b>, Wang Jinghong and others gave the order for the first Ming treasure voyage.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-11">11 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
